import Mathlib
/-! QED-E180: Is the two-generator Burnside group of exponent 5 finite?
Take the 'freest' group generated by two elements a and b in which every element g satisfies g^5 = 1. Is this group finite? For exponents 2, 3, 4 and 6 the answer is yes; for large exponents it is infinite; exponent 5 with two generators is the smallest case nobody can decide. If it is finite, it must have exactly 5^34 elements.
Informal statement: Let $F_2$ be the free group on $\{a,b\}$ and $N=\langle\!\langle g^5: g\in F_2\rangle\!\rangle$. Decide whether $B(2,5)=F_2/N$ is finite. (Equivalently, since its largest finite quotient $R(2,5)$ has order $5^{34}$: decide whether $|B(2,5)|=5^{34}$.)
Formalization notes: `B25` is Mathlib's `PresentedGroup` on two generators (`Fin 2`) with relators all fifth powers h⁵, h ∈ FreeGroup (Fin 2). By definition `PresentedGroup rels` is `FreeGroup α ⧸ Subgroup.normalClosure rels`, so this is literally F₂/N (the relator set is already normal, so N is just the subgroup it generates, but this does not matter). `Claim` = B(2,5) is finite (then, by Kostrikin and Havas–Wall–Wamsley, it has order 5³⁴; that value is not part of `Claim`). `¬Claim` = B(2,5) is infinite. Either settles the problem.
-/
namespace OpenProblems.QEDE180

/-- The free Burnside group of rank 2 and exponent 5. -/
def B25 : Type :=
  PresentedGroup {g : FreeGroup (Fin 2) | ∃ h : FreeGroup (Fin 2), g = h ^ 5}

/-- `B(2,5)` is finite. -/
def Claim : Prop := Finite B25

end OpenProblems.QEDE180
